From 1ca6f26cbea0ca6b06f19f4fd56d5c89c7f1710b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: whn09 <wanghenan09@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 15 Sep 2026 18:57:34 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 12/12] vdn: serve N requests from one process, and cycle the
 decoders rather than the DiT

Every latency figure in RESULTS.md was one render per process with the decoder
load subtracted off, and that subtraction is wrong in both directions at once.
`render.repeat=N` serves N complete requests -- denoise, decode, encode -- from
the one warm process and reports the distribution. Re-running the *process* N
times is not the same test: it re-measures the 220 s build every time and never
lets two requests share a resident model, which is the entire question a server
asks.

It is also the only leak detector available. At 768p the decode peak clears the
non-PyTorch floor by about 1 GiB, so a few hundred MiB of growth per request
would pass a one-shot render and fail the fourth one.

What it found, immediately:

  * 480p steady state is 11.45 s +/- 0.040, not the published 13.20. The
    correction is systematic, not noise: a process's FIRST request is slower in
    stages that have nothing to do with the decoder load -- video VAE 2.12 s then
    1.55 s forever after, tail 1.56 s then 1.18 s -- and subtracting a one-time
    cost from a first render leaves all of those in. The +/- 0.6 s this file
    attributed to the video VAE was between-process variance; in one process that
    stage has an sd of 1 ms, and it is the same 1.56 s at 345 and 362 frames,
    which is the ceil(chunks/8) model landing exactly.

  * 768p `keep` on both sides does not survive a second request. The previous
    commit made a 768p render hold the DiT and reported 26.79 s steady, and that
    claim was false: the decoders are loaded AFTER the first denoise, so that
    denoise never coexists with them and every later one does. Request 2 dies in
    the denoise on the three linear-branch ranks, 444-468 MiB short.

So something has to cycle per request at 768p, and `parallel.vae_after_decode`
picks which side. The arithmetic is measured rather than assumed: per rank the
DiT is 45.24 GiB, the video VAE 9.70 (audio 0.564, rank 0 only), the non-PyTorch
floor ~13.6, and the denoise wants ~16.9 GiB of activations in the 10.6 that are
left. Cycling the decoders moves 4.7x less data than cycling the weights, so
`free` drops them after the decode and the existing `model.vae is None` guard
reloads them. Ten requests at 768p: 33.21 s +/- 0.850, memory growth +0.000 GiB.
480p cycles nothing and stays `keep` at both ends.

Two implementation notes that are less obvious than they look:

  * `timings` IS `requests[0]`, so the list goes in the record alongside it and
    never nested inside it -- nesting makes the tree self-referential and json
    refuses to encode it. Which it did, after ten good requests. For the same
    reason write_json now runs after every print: a ten-request run is 15 minutes
    of GPU time, and serialisation is the one step here that can fail on data
    rather than on memory, so it must not be able to take the numbers with it.

  * request 0 stays THE record. Every existing consumer -- scripts/summarize.py,
    the h100_grid.sh greps, every arm already in RESULTS.md -- reads the same
    fields off a repeat=1 run and off request 0 of a repeat=10 run.

Left on the table, designed and not built: a pinned host copy of the 9.70 GiB
video VAE restores at the measured 10.08 GiB/s in ~0.96 s against the 5.05 s disk
load, which would take 768p from 33.21 s to ~28. And 768p's steady-state denoise
is 1.2 s slower than its first (19.76 -> 20.97), with two candidate causes this
run cannot separate: allocator churn from cycling 9.70 GiB through empty_cache()
every request, or sustained-load clocks. 480p's denoise moves +0.03 s over ten
requests, which controls for the first and not for the second.

