+    # What to do with the DECODERS once the frames are out, which is the mirror image of
+    # transformer_before_decode and, at 768p, the one that actually matters.
+    #
+    #   "keep"  leave them resident. Correct at 480p, and correct at 768p for a ONE-SHOT
+    #           render -- but at 768p it does not survive a second request, which a single
+    #           render cannot show and render.repeat can. Measured: request 1 completes, then
+    #           request 2's denoise dies on the three linear-branch ranks 444-468 MiB short.
+    #           The decoders are loaded AFTER the first denoise, so that denoise never coexists
+    #           with them; every later one does.
+    #   "free"  drop them after the decode and reload before the next one. Costs a decoder load
+    #           per request and is the only thing at 768p that makes the DiT's residency real.
+    #
+    # Which side to cycle is settled by measurement, not preference: the video VAE is 9.70 GiB
+    # per rank (audio 0.564, rank 0 only) against the DiT's 45.24. The card gives PyTorch
+    # 65.26 GiB (79.18 total minus a measured 13.92 non-PyTorch floor), keeping both leaves
+    # 65.26 - 54.94 = 10.32 GiB, and the 768p denoise's activations peak at 10.3-10.6 on a
+    # linear-branch rank -- so it misses by a few hundred MiB, which is why one request passes
+    # and the second does not. Cycling the decoders moves 4.7x less data than cycling the
+    # weights. (The decode's own activations are the smaller of the two stages, ~8.1 GiB, once
+    # stream_yuv_assembly and the per-slot gather are on; before them the decode was the
+    # binding stage instead.)
+    vae_after_decode: str = "keep"
 
 
